# mate-commands

Type the binary's absolute path, `/usr/local/bin/mate`, from your own working directory; it finds the workspace by walking up, so you pass `--workspace` only from outside it.
For any command's syntax, run `/usr/local/bin/mate <command> --help`; never guess a flag.
Exit 2 is a usage mistake of yours, exit 1 a refusal or a real failure, with the reason on stderr.
A `note:` or `warning:` on stderr is information, not failure.
A refusal changes nothing unless it says so: fix what it names and run the same command again. Never route around a refusal with a stronger flag.

## Project facts

`mate project facts <project>` says what each repo holds without you reading it: whether its default branch has a commit, its head, its file count, the names at its top level, its build, test and doc files by name. It reads git's record of the committed tree and opens no file.
A repo whose default branch is empty answers:

```text
shop: repo shop at /ws/shop, default branch main
commits: 0 (main has no commit yet)
head: none (anchor shop:main@none)
tree: empty
note: names and counts from the committed tree of main only; no file was opened, and uncommitted changes in the checkout are not seen
```

A populated one prints `head: <sha> (anchor <repo>:<branch>@<sha>)` and the tree's names, one block per repo.
Cite it as a source in `## What we already know`. The `(anchor ...)` is exactly what a PROJECT.md fact about that repo carries, `<repo>:<branch>@<sha>`. A file's name says nothing about its content, so it never replaces a scout.

## Recall

`mate recall <project>` prints your session-start digest in numbered parts, live state first and memory last, so a cut digest never loses the crews:

1. Live state: the mode, the open crews, every unresolved inbox item, and your outbox.
2. `mate project facts`: one block per repo.
3. `/ws/.mate/projects/shop/PROJECT.md`, then a warning for each anchor its repo has moved past.
4. `/ws/.mate/projects/shop/mate/backlog.md` without `## Done` itself, then the In flight ids that are not open crews and the open crews with no line; with a tracker plugin, what the tracker says of each pointer.
5. `/ws/.mate/projects/shop/mate/memory.md`.
6. `/ws/.mate/WORKSPACE.md`, and whether the two CREW.md files have rules.
7. What `memory check` says.
8. Only when the timeline database exists: lines you sent crews and questions crews asked in the last 14 days, and how full your context is. Hints for `stow`, never memory.

A missing file prints `ABSENT` and an empty one `(empty)`, which mean different things.
`--live` prints part 1 only. `--part <n>[,<n>...]` reprints whole parts. `--max-bytes <n>` cuts from the bottom, part 1 always survives whole, and the last line names what was left out.

## Refusals

**`crew spawn`** creates nothing when it refuses.

- A brief that fails the check lists every problem: fix the file and run the same spawn.
- `has no repo yet`, or an unknown `--repo`: add the repo or pick one from the list it prints.
- `invalid crew dispatch table`: every spawn is refused until the captain fixes the file; tell them the line and keep the task queued.
- `--deliver pr` refused: `gh` is not logged in or `origin` is not on GitHub; tell the captain what it says, or deliver locally.
- `--model` or `--effort` without `--harness` (exit 2): a model belongs to one harness; pass the profile whole.

**`crew stop`** refuses a branch not landed in its repo's default branch, or a dirty worktree, and leaves the Crew running.
That is a refusal to destroy work: get the branch merged, or ask the captain whether to throw it away.

**`mate merge`** prints `merge refused:` and moves nothing. `<default>` is the default branch of the Crew's repo.

- `the Mate merges only reviewed work` - run `mate review` first.
- `review is stale` - the branch, pull request or contract moved after the review: review again (`review-delivery`).
- `passed but left N finding(s) standing` - record them (backlog `## Queued` or the tracker) and merge again with `--followups`.
- `has N uncommitted file(s)` naming the Crew's worktree - send the Crew one line to commit or drop them; never merge around them.
- `needs-rebase` - `<default>` moved on: send the Crew one line to rebase onto `<default>` and hand back. It is never a state of the Crew.
- `is not ahead of <default>: nothing to merge` - the Crew committed nothing; report it as such.
- `has <branch> checked out, not <default>` or uncommitted files in the repo's own checkout - the captain is working there; say so and stop.
- `has no pull request recorded` - the Crew has not opened one; tell it.
- `is closed, not open` or `is merged, not open` - nothing to merge; a merged one needs `crew stop`.
- `gh could not merge <url>` - read the reason, send the Crew one line to fix it on the same branch, and merge after its next hand-back.

**`brief append`** refuses a Crew with no live agent and leaves the brief alone.
When the brief changed but the send was refused, stderr prints the exact `mate send` line to run once the Crew is free: run that, never `brief append` again, or the words land twice.

**`mate send`** refusals are in `crew-recovery`.

**`mate review --since`** refuses a reviewer with no record, one for another Crew, one with no verdict yet, a branch still at the commit it saw (use `--check`), and a branch rewritten past that commit (review in full).

**`mate remember`** refuses (exit 2) an absolute source, a path outside the workspace, a second line or a parenthesis in the source, and writes nothing.

## Console editor setup

When the captain asks to set up an editor, change its key or fix its launch:

1. Run `/usr/local/bin/mate console --help` for the config schema and examples; it opens no console.
2. Read `/ws/.mate/console.yaml` if it exists, and check the editor's CLI. The config lives at the workspace root, not in your cwd.
3. For an authorized change, keep every other binding and change only the requested one: `external` for a GUI launcher, `terminal` for a host tab, the command as argv. `{report_path}` needs an existing report; `{crew_dir}` opens the Crew's folder.
4. Tell the captain the file, key and target row, and have them reopen the console and press the key. The editor works only once that key press opens it.

## Task tracker

The captain chooses a tracker through `/ws/.mate/WORKSPACE.md`, `/ws/.mate/projects/shop/PROJECT.md` or a user skill; mate has no built-in binding.
When configuring one, record its absolute location, command or skill, workflow states and who updates it in the captain's instructions. Run its commands where it lives, read its workflow before using it, keep task ids in briefs, and update delivery after acceptance.
A board shortcut is a project-scoped command in `/ws/.mate/console.yaml`, following `console --help`; the key is the captain's choice.

## Plugins

Plugins are installed per workspace, by the captain: `mate plugin add <package-path> --as <instance-id> [--set key=value ...] [--key <key>]`, without `--project`; every project's Mate receives it at its next start. Give that command when asked.
`mate plugin doctor [<instance-id>]` checks them offline and `mate plugin list` shows them.
You never log in to a plugin: after a change and a restart, the captain runs `/mcp` in your pane, and only one live read through its tools proves it works.
